On Apr 20, 4:12 pm, SvN <s...@alum.mit.edu> wrote:
> This was my very first ascension - it feels good to finally make it.
> Just for the record, it was with 6363966 points.  At my peak I had AC
> -24, HP 364 Pwr 269 Str 18/**

That's actually fairly bad AC for a first ascension.  In fact,
even now, I'd want it higher for anything but a Valk.  (I'm
reading a Sam for an ascention run right now.  At the moment, AC
is only -22, but it will be higher before I attack Rodney: I've
still got three unused wishes, a magic marker, and lots of junk
scrolls to blank.)

> So what did I do that made it easy?
>  - lots of genocides - basically 3 blessed scrolls to take out all
> mind-flayers, vampires and eel-like things. That was basically a full
> wand of wishing right there.

Vampires and the eel-like things really aren't worth it.
Mind-flayers maybe.  But I'd get rid of liches first.  (On the
other hand, genocide isn't really that imporant.  My second
ascension was genocideless, not because I'd decided to do a
conduct, but simply because by the time I found my first scroll
of genocide, I was far enough along that it didn't seem worth
the bother; it seems a better strategy to keep the scroll, in
case I got into a bind later.)

>  - lots of sacrificing on aligned altars (and converted a few to get
> more).

Which gets you whay?  Artifacts that you won't use?  As a Valk
or Sam, I tend to sacrifice very little---just enough to know
whether it's safe to pray.  (Of course, that sometimes results
in an artifact anyway.  If it's Grayswandir, fine.  Otherwise,
into the nearest chest, just so that no monster will use it on
me.)

>  - paid lots of protection money to priests, including everything I
> looted from Fort Ludios.

Which stops paying after a certain point.

>  - finally learned (late in the game) about how to use foocubuses.
>  - didn't use a single healing-type potion unless I was at full health
>  - ate a lot of giants.
>  - wore a ring of conflict pretty much non-stop for the last half of
> the game. (No, not many pets.)

I'm not sure that's a good idea either.  I only put on a ring of
conflict when there is a group to deal with.  In practice, this
means the planes, and a few specific places elsewhere.
Otherwise, I would forget to take it off when I went into a shop
or a temple.

>  - I've seen very few samurai make it. I guess if I'd known that at
> the start I might have played a different character.

I think Samurai is one of the easier characters to ascend.  The
only tricky part is the quest, since the quest artifact can be
very dangerous in the hands of the quest nemisis.  (On the other
hand, the quest nemisis doesn't seem sleep resistant, so a wand
of sleep, then wack away, does the trick.  At least, that's what
I did this time: two weaponing a +6 Grayswandir and a +4 katana,
he never woke up.)

>  - got as good as I could at spellcasting. Nothing worked more than
> 42% of the time, but learned over 30 spells. (Gotta love fireball,
> cone of cold, speed self and charm monster)

The most useful, IMHO, are cure sickness, remove curse and
jumping; I like to have cure sickness at 100% if possible,
remove curse continues to be useful even at 50% failurer rate.
And even without a robe, you can get a lot of the spells down to
30% failure rate.  (With a robe, you can get most of them down
to 0% failure.)

And yes, even with fighter classes, I like to cast spells too.

>  - 3 luckstones (just in case)

One's enough:-).

> What did I do that might have made it harder?
>  - polymorphed no objects.
>  - wished for no artifacts (Still managed to get Sunsword (+0),
> Firebrand (+1), Excalibur (duh!) (+4), Snickersnee (+4 but never
> used), Tsurugi of Muramasa (+4), Dragonbane (+0, almost never used),
> Werebane (+0, just for demons)) Mostly just slugged away with
> Excalibur and my Tsurugi. Maybe I should have boosted Firebrand
> instead...

I'd use Excalibur over all of the others there.  Two weaponed
with a silver sabre.  About the only thing I'd use in preference
would be Grayswandir (if I happened to get it), two weaponned
with a katana.

>  - no metal armor - decided to maximize my spellcasting. Mostly used
> +5 gray dragon, +5 hawaiian shirt, +1 cloak of protection, +2 boots of
> stealth.

Given GDSM, I'd definitely have tried to replace that cloak of
protection with a robe.  And the boots of stealth with speed or
jumping.  A helm of brilliance can also improve spell casting.
And you do want gloves; wielding a cockatrice corpse in a croud
can be fun.

>  - no launchers - gave up on arrows/ya's early and focused on spells
> and wands for ranged attacks.

If you've got enough wands, fine.

>  - no life-saving amulets - amulet of reflection was just too sweet.

You generally want reflection.  You can two weapon, so you don't
want a shield; that leaves two possibilities: the amulet or
SDSM.  With SDSM, you need magic protection from somewhere else.

>  - almost no pets. Ring of conflict too sweet. On the Astral plane I
> could wade through crowded rooms taking basically no damage aside from
> the three riders.

Pets are important in the early stages.  Later, if you can get a
good one (polymorph), which can take out priests, they can help
you get a robe.  Beyond that, however, I'm not convinced.

